helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\00000002.xhp	0	help	tit				0	hi	$[officename] Basic Glossary				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\00000002.xhp	0	help	hd_id3145068				0	hi	\<link href=\"text/sbasic/shared/00000002.xhp\" name=\"$[officename] Basic Glossary\"\>$[officename] Basic Glossary\</link\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\00000002.xhp	0	help	par_id3150792				0	hi	This glossary explains some technical terms that you may come across when working with $[officename] Basic.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\00000002.xhp	0	help	hd_id3155133				0	hi	दशमलव बिंदु				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\00000002.xhp	0	help	par_id3156443				0	hi	When converting numbers, $[officename] Basic uses the locale settings of the system for determining the type of decimal and thousand separator.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\00000002.xhp	0	help	par_id3153092				0	hi	The behavior has an effect on both the implicit conversion ( 1 + "2.3" = 3.3 ) as well as the runtime function \<link href=\"text/sbasic/shared/03102700.xhp\" name=\"IsNumeric\"\>IsNumeric\</link\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\00000002.xhp	0	help	hd_id3155854				0	hi	रंग				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\00000002.xhp	0	help	par_id3145366				0	hi	In $[officename] Basic, colors are treated as long integer value. The return value of color queries is also always a long integer value. When defining properties, colors can be specified using their RGB code that is converted to a long integer value using the \<link href=\"text/sbasic/shared/03010305.xhp\" name=\"RGB function\"\>RGB function\</link\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\00000002.xhp	0	help	hd_id3146119				0	hi	मापक इकाईयां				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\00000002.xhp	0	help	par_id3154013				0	hi	In $[officename] Basic, a \<emph\>method parameter\</emph\> or a \<emph\>property\</emph\> expecting unit information can be specified either as integer or long integer expression without a unit, or as a character string containing a unit. If no unit is passed to the method the default unit defined for the active document type will be used. If the parameter is passed as a character string containing a measurement unit, the default setting will be ignored. The default measurement unit for a document type can be set under \<emph\>\<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"MAC\"\>%PRODUCTNAME - Preferences\</caseinline\>\<defaultinline\>Tools - Options\</defaultinline\>\</switchinline\> - (Document Type) - General\</emph\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\00000002.xhp	0	help	bm_id3145801				0	hi	\<bookmark_value\>twips; definition\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\00000002.xhp	0	help	hd_id3145801				0	hi	ट्विप्स				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\00000002.xhp	0	help	par_id3154731				0	hi	A twip is a screen-independent unit which is used to define the uniform position and size of screen elements on all display systems. A twip is 1/1440th of an inch or 1/20 of a printer's point. There are 1440 twips to an inch or about 567 twips to a centimeter.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\00000002.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153159				0	hi	यूआरएल नोटेशन				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\00000002.xhp	0	help	par_id3153415				0	hi	URLs (\<emph\>Uniform Resource Locators\</emph\>) are used to determine the location of a resource like a file in a file system, typically inside a network environment. A URL consists of a protocol specifier, a host specifier and a file and path specifier:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\00000002.xhp	0	help	par_id3149121				0	hi	\<emph\>protocol\</emph\>://\<emph\>host.name\</emph\>/\<emph\>path/to/the/file.html\</emph\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\00000002.xhp	0	help	par_id3168612				0	hi	The most common usage of URLs is on the internet when specifying web pages. Example for protocols are \<emph\>http\</emph\>, \<emph\>ftp\</emph\>, or \<emph\>file\</emph\>. The \<emph\>file\</emph\> protocol specifier is used when referring to a file on the local file system.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\00000002.xhp	0	help	par_id3150324	110			0	hi	URL notation does not allow certain special characters to be used. These are either replaced by other characters or encoded. A slash (\<emph\>/\</emph\>) is used as a path separator. For example, a file referred to as \<emph\>C:\\My File.sxw\</emph\> on the local host in "Windows notation" becomes \<emph\>file:///C|/My%20File.sxw\</emph\> in URL notation.				20200411 15:09:53
