helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\text.xhp	0	help	hd_id8509170				0	hi	How to enter direct text strings that do not get interpreted?				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\text.xhp	0	help	par_id337229				0	hi	Some text strings get interpreted as operators automatically. Sometimes this is not what you want. If you want to write W\<emph\>*\</emph\> (a letter with a superscripted asterisk), the asterisk will be interpreted as a multiplication operator. Enclose the direct text within double quotes or add spaceholders.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\text.xhp	0	help	par_idN105D0				0	hi	उदाहरण:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\text.xhp	0	help	par_id521866				0	hi	An imported MathType formula contains the following string				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\text.xhp	0	help	par_id9413739				0	hi	W rSup { size 8{*} }				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\text.xhp	0	help	par_id755943				0	hi	If you have set up Math to convert imported MathType formulas (in \<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"MAC\"\>%PRODUCTNAME - Preferences\</caseinline\>\<defaultinline\>Tools - Options\</defaultinline\>\</switchinline\> - Load/Save - Microsoft Office), you see the formula with a placeholder instead of the asterisk.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\text.xhp	0	help	par_id5988952				0	hi	Change {*} to {} * {} as in the following formula:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\text.xhp	0	help	par_id3304544				0	hi	W rSup { size 8{} * {} }				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\text.xhp	0	help	par_id4941557				0	hi	You can also use W^"*" to enter the character as direct text.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\text.xhp	0	help	par_id9961851				0	hi	Some formulas start with an = sign. Use "=" to enter that character as direct text.				20200411 15:09:53
