helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03090500.xhp	0	help	par_id3149623				0	hi	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_SMA_SLRFLOORX\"\>To insert scalable floor brackets, type \<emph\>left lfloor<?>right rfloor\</emph\> directly in the \<emph\>Commands\</emph\> window.\</ahelp\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03090500.xhp	0	help	par_id3145668				0	hi	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_SMA_SLRCEILX\"\>To insert scalable ceiling brackets, type \<emph\>left lceil<?>right rceil\</emph\> directly in the \<emph\>Commands\</emph\> window.\</ahelp\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03090500.xhp	0	help	par_id3149208				0	hi	Brackets are automatically sized when you type \<emph\>left\</emph\> and \<emph\>right\</emph\> in front of the bracket command, for example, \<emph\>left(a over b right)\</emph\>. You can also set the size and spacing of brackets by choosing \<emph\>Format - Spacing - Category - Brackets\</emph\> and setting the desired percentages. Mark the \<emph\>Scale all brackets\</emph\> check box to apply the changes to all brackets in the formula.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03090500.xhp	0	help	par_id3150857				0	hi	You can also use single brackets. To do this, type a backslash \<emph\>\\\</emph\> in front of the command. For example, when you type \<emph\>\\[\</emph\>, the left square bracket appears without its counterpart. This is useful for creating reverse brackets or for constructing intervals. Note that only non-scalable brackets can be used individually. To change the size, use the \<emph\>size\</emph\> command.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03090500.xhp	0	help	par_idN113E5				0	hi	एकल कोष्ठकों के उदाहरण				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03090500.xhp	0	help	par_idN113E8				0	hi	आकार नहीं बदले जा सकने वाले कोष्ठकों के लिए:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03090500.xhp	0	help	par_idN113EB				0	hi	a = \\{ \\( \\[ b newline				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03090500.xhp	0	help	par_idN113EE				0	hi	{} + c \\] \\) \\ }				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03090500.xhp	0	help	par_idN113F3				0	hi	For scaled brackets use \<emph\>none\</emph\> as the bracket name				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03090500.xhp	0	help	par_idN113FA				0	hi	a = left ( a over b right none newline				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03090500.xhp	0	help	par_idN113FF				0	hi	left none phantom {a over b} + c right )				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03090500.xhp	0	help	par_idN11404				0	hi	The \<emph\>phantom\</emph\> statement ensures that the last bracket is the correct size.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03090500.xhp	0	help	par_id3145107				0	hi	Be sure to put spaces (gaps) between elements when entering them directly in the Commands window. This ensures that the correct structure is recognized.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03090500.xhp	0	help	par_id3153198				0	hi	Useful information about \<link href=\"text/smath/01/03091200.xhp\" name=\"indexes and exponents\"\>indexes and exponents\</link\> as well as \<link href=\"text/smath/01/03091400.xhp\" name=\"scaling\"\>scaling\</link\> helps you structure formulas effectively. For more information about brackets, see \<link href=\"text/smath/01/03091100.xhp\" name=\"Brackets and Groups\"\>Brackets and Grouping\</link\>.				20200411 15:09:53
